**Current support.** Moth supports template-head directives and the `$config` source modifier. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

A directive modifies an existing construct. Template-head directives modify their template and source directives modify the following eligible construct.

### Where directives go

A directive in a template head modifies the containing template. For example, $md formats the template body as Markdown:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    message #= [$md: Hello from Moth]
]

The target is the template whose head contains the directive, not an enclosing template. Other template directives declare slots, insert content and control formatting. See @../templates/#template-directives (Template directives).

A source directive modifies the next eligible declaration or statement. The compiler defines which kinds of constructs each directive can modify. `$config` marks one explicitly typed top-level compile-time `#` binding as a build input:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"
]

The binding name is the input key, its declared type is the receiving contract and its initializer is the missing-input fallback. Only a marked binding may omit its initializer. See @../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build configuration values).

Source directives use their own line. The next eligible construct must follow without an empty line. Comments may appear between the directive and its target as long as no blank line intervenes:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    -- Default server address.
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"
]

An empty line, even one containing only spaces, breaks attachment. A directive also cannot cross another declaration, a scope boundary or the end of a file. Multiple compatible modifiers can appear together without blank lines. A directive at the beginning of a file still modifies the following construct.

### Compile-time operands

A directive may accept one compile-time operand, with either a value or a named value. Its permitted form depends on the directive:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    $name
    $name value
    $name key = value
]

A named operand contains one key and one value. It is not a function parameter list, and it cannot have an authored type annotation. Values use Moth's compile-time expression rules and cannot depend on runtime state or cause side effects. `$config` accepts no operand.

The compiler registers `$before`, `$after`, `$fresh`, `$slot`, `$in`, `$note`, `$todo`, `$doc`, `$raw`, `$md`, `$html`, `$css`, `$escape_html` and `$code` for template heads, and `$config` as the source-only modifier.

Directive calls such as `$name(...)` are rejected with a placement error rather than parsed as a call. Ordinary function calls such as `f()` are unaffected.

Template heads retain their own separators and slot labels. For example, `$slot title` names a slot, `$slot title = "Untitled"` supplies a default and `$in title` contributes to that slot. `$code "moth"`, `$css "inline"`, `$before value` and `$after value` are single-operand forms. These forms do not alter how those directives compose templates.

### Module roots and configuration

Module roles come from filenames rather than directives. A directory can contain @mod.moth for its normal public API, @package.moth for its support package API and @entry.moth for its executable entry. The presence of @entry.moth makes the entry eligible for builder selection. No directive marks a module or file as an entry.

The separate config.moth file uses ordinary named constants for project and builder settings and the `$config` source modifier for typed inputs:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    version #String? = "1.0.0"

    project #= (
        name = "my_site",
        version = version,
    )

    html #= ()
]

`$config` attaches to one explicitly typed top-level compile-time `#` binding. Its name is the input key, its declared type is the contract and its initializer is the fallback. Only a marked binding may omit an initializer. Other top-level names remain ordinary private config helper constants.

There are no $project, $builder or $html_builder directives. See @../project-structure/#project-config (Project configuration) and @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).

### Invalid directives

The compiler checks a directive's name and its permitted target. A known directive in the wrong context should receive a placement error rather than an unknown-name error. For example, $md belongs inside a template head, while $config belongs before an eligible source declaration.

A source directive without a target, separated from its target by a blank line or repeated where only one is allowed is an error. Diagnostics identify the directive and, where useful, the conflicting or invalid target. LF and CRLF line endings follow the same attachment rules.

Directives are compiler-defined. Moth code cannot define new directives, and project builders cannot register arbitrary syntax or semantic callbacks. A directive cannot act as a macro that rewrites another construct.

Future source directives such as $export, $test and numeric constraints will have their own eligible targets. The planned $export modifier is restricted to declarations in @mod.moth and @package.moth, not @entry.moth or ordinary helper files. This page defines the common syntax, not the implementation of those future features.

